      ":question/id": "b63080c7-3378-5dc4-b6ed-329a0204656c",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $\\lambda (-18\\mathbf{a} + 9\\mathbf{b})$ and $-24\\mathbf{a} + 12\\mathbf{b}$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $\\lambda .$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Since the vectors are equal, we can write\n\n$$\n-24\\mathbf{a} + 12\\mathbf{b}=\\lambda (-18\\mathbf{a} + 9\\mathbf{b}).\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the left-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}(-18\\mathbf{a} + 9\\mathbf{b})=\\lambda (-18\\mathbf{a} + 9\\mathbf{b}).\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $(-18\\mathbf{a} + 9\\mathbf{b})$ on both sides. So, let's define a new vector $u$ as\n\n$$\nu=-18\\mathbf{a} + 9\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nThen, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}\\,u=\\lambda u.\n$$\n\nSince $u$ is non-zero,\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$",
